version: "2.0"
name: gnomon-kernel-loops
description: >-
  Loops in the kernel: the loop registry and trust, the gate that admits a loop
  run, the runner that walks a loop graph, check execution and parsing, the
  propose (act) step, landing, timers, loop events, the strictness cap and
  agent-facing loop operations.
context:
  - >-
    registry.ts, trust.ts and gate.ts decide what may run; runner.ts,
    check-exec.ts, propose.ts and land.ts run it; timers.ts, events.ts,
    strictness-cap.ts and agent-ops.ts surround it.
  - >-
    The loop file schema lives in src/loops/ (#loop-schema); the loop git
    scratch lives in src/kernel/git/ (#loop-git); the ^loop-trusted gate stays
    in packages/gnomon/.purpose.
  - >-
    Split out of packages/gnomon/.purpose (2026-10-27); path: values stay
    relative to packages/gnomon, the package root.
components:
  loop-registry:
    description: >-
      #loop-registry (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.1, §4, §5; build plan LP2)
      in src/kernel/loops/{registry,hash,trust,wire}.ts and src/cli/loop-cli.ts.
      Registry: watches compiled .paradigm/loops/*.loop.json in known repos
      (from kernel-signed session.open{cwd}, live sessions, or a human loop
      request naming the repo) and <home>/loops/*.loop.json (personal; refused
      when a symlink, group/world-writable or another user's); lstat on every
      dir and file, symlinks refused, size-capped; every file re-validated
      (validateCompiledLoop), never trusted. Content hash (hash.ts) = sha256
      of canonicalJson {def: sha256(canonicalJson(parsed file)), argv: per run
      check argv0 as resolveArgv0 finds it (path word vs repo root, bare name
      in <repo>/node_modules/.bin then the session PATH) with realpath+sha256
      and its shebang interpreter (`/usr/bin/env node` = the runner's node,
      process.execPath), every file an argv word names in or out of the repo,
      calls: each pin + the callee's CURRENT hash, closure: digest of the
      gate-class files (manifest/layers.ts gateClassPathRule file/test/code/env
      rules + tsconfig*) in the repo root and on the way to each argv path};
      coreHash omits the closure. The kernel's protected set adds callees' and
      closure paths. State is a fold of kernel-signed loop.trusted|untrusted|
      closure.changed|paused|resumed|deleted rows keyed {loopId, scope,
      repoKey}: untrusted, trusted, waiting-trust, paused, deleted, invalid. A
      trusted loop whose hash moves gets ONE loop.paused{content-changed}; a
      closure-only move asks the origin (gitClosureOrigin: uncommitted or
      Gnomon-Session/Loop-Authored-By = agent, Gnomon-Loop or
      @gnomon.invalid = loop, else human): loop/agent write
      loop.closure.changed{by} + loop.paused{closure-changed}, a human commit
      only loop.closure.changed{by:human} and the trust moves to the new hash.
      resolveCall(): callee in scope, TRUSTED at its current hash, pin =
      its trusted sourceHash. Trust (trust.ts): request.loop.trust{loopId,
      scope, repo|repoKey, contentHash?} raises ONE loop-trust card (root
      class, handOnly, never always/standing) rendering every node, argv0
      realpath+sha and interpreter, argv files, tools, model, triggers,
      budgets, goal/protected inputs, land, call pins, closure, and a line
      diff vs the trusted version's snapshot (<home>/loop-trust/<hex>.json);
      allow re-checks the hash under beginRootDecision and signs ONE
      loop.trusted{loopId, scope, repoKey, contentHash, coreHash,
      closureDigest, sourceHash, cardId, by} row with the root key in the same
      touch (decision-batch.ts and Protocol.swift row table `loop-trust`); the
      effect appends it as a kernel row with rootSig. request.loop.untrust is a
      tightening (no presence, loop.untrusted{by}); request.loop.delete writes
      loop.deleted (a personal file is removed; repo files stay);
      pause/resume/run are #loop-runner's (wire.ts routes them). Wiring: one wireLoops()
      in daemon.ts, one request.loop.* block in dispatch.ts, status.loops for
      the human. CLI: gnomon loop list|show|trust|untrust|trust-status
      (run|pause|resume|runs|logs are #loop-runner's). trust.ts exports
      parseLoopTarget, the one request.loop.* target parser.
      TD-2026-10-23-268/448: the trust card (envCardLines) says "uses your
      env", "reads env from:" every source in lookup order with its real path
      and place (repo, tracked by git, outside this repo), one "NAME ← source"
      line per name (or its candidates), the network warning when env and
      network are both on, "shows check output unredacted", "checks can reach
      the network", and under a call node the callee's env names, files and
      network (calleeEnv). All but the sources are in the compiled definition
      and the content hash. Repo env files a loop links leave its config
      closure and join its protected set; editing a value needs no re-trust.
      LoopRunRefInfo.nodeEnv carries the trusted node's env names (Aegis F4).
      TD-2026-10-24-447 (#loops-gate): with loops off, wireLoops() returns the
      inert disabledLoops() stub and constructs no registry, runner, timers,
      watchers or idle holder; `gnomon loop *` prints the same copy and exits 1.
    path: src/kernel/loops/registry.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, security]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-registry.test.ts
      - test/loop-compiled.test.ts
      - test/loop-cli.test.ts
      - test/decision-batch.test.ts
  loops-gate:
    description: >-
      #loops-gate (TD-2026-10-24-447; docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §11, G-LOOP-29) in
      src/kernel/loops/gate.ts. The one boot-time switch for loops, resolved once in
      daemon.ts before wireLoops(): a release build (the compiled __GNOMON_RELEASE__
      define, via loadedReleasePins) is OFF; a dev build is ON unless GNOMON_LOOPS=0;
      GNOMON_LOOPS=1 never turns it on in a release build. Never read from a user
      file or the log. Off: wireLoops() returns the disabledLoops() stub (no registry,
      runner, timers, watchers, idle hold, exit hook; no log seeding) whose
      request/agent refuse FORBIDDEN loops-disabled "Loops are off in this build.
      They arrive in Gnomon 8.1." and whose status() is {enabled:false,
      reason:'not-in-this-build', loops:[], ...}; the stub stays wired as
      requests.loops so request.loop.* and loop.request never fall to generic
      log.append. dispatch.ts also refuses both blocks when status().enabled is
      false, before any row. One diag daemon.desk loops.disabled at boot. Existing
      loop rows and files stay (append-only), inert. $daemon-lifecycle owns the boot call.
    path: src/kernel/loops/gate.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, release]
    gates: []
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loops-gate.test.ts
      - test/loops-gate-daemon.test.ts
      - test/release-darwin.test.ts
  loop-check:
    description: >-
      #loop-check (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 check nodes, §4 Sandboxes and
      Evidence, §7; build plan step 4; TD-2026-10-22-129: the loops' own
      read-only profile in place of M2.3's gnomon-oracle, G:1596). Profile:
      src/kernel/sandbox/loop-check.ts (LOOP_CHECK_PROFILE, registry
      LOOP_CHECK_BACKENDS = {darwin}; win32 is absent for W5 and, like linux,
      refuses sandbox-unavailable; darwinFloor macOS 15+ and SIP;
      assertNoProtectedGrant against $HOME, the Gnomon home, keys/ and
      LOOP_CHECK_CREDENTIAL_STORES) and src/platform/darwin/loop-check-sbpl.ts
      (default-deny: exec literals of the kernel node, the hashed argv0 and its
      shebang interpreter only, fork allowed; read the run dir, the linked
      node_modules (write-denied), the Node runtime and the pinned paradigm CLI
      package; write the run dir only; NO network rule; the Gnomon home and
      credential stores read/write-denied last). Executor:
      src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts runCheck. git ls-files -co
      --exclude-standard (core.fsmonitor off) copied into the KERNEL-OWNED
      run dir <home>/loop-work/check-<runId>/{repo,home,tmp} (0700, under the
      Gnomon home, never inside the repo; the profile denies the home and
      re-allows only the run dir after it). Every parent lstat'd (no links),
      files read through O_NOFOLLOW fds for at most their lstat size, then
      the parent chain (dev:ino), file identity and realpath containment
      re-checked: a swap refuses the whole copy (Aegis step-4 review). Caps
      50 000 files, half a gigabyte, 500 node_modules links (copy-too-large);
      node_modules linked read-only only when its realpath is inside the repo
      (else copy-failed); env from runner/env.ts buildCheckEnv (G-SEC-02);
      argv exec'd directly in its own process group, timeoutSec then SIGTERM,
      SIGKILL after 2 s and a group SIGKILL after every exit, all through
      process-control; the tree is polled (a snapshot every half second) and descendants
      that left the group (setsid) are SIGKILLed by pid + start witness
      (escapedKilled). argv0 (copy-mapped inside the repo) re-hashed against
      its pin; a script argv0 needs a pinned, unchanged interpreter
      (src/kernel/loops/shebang.ts, the one shebang parser shared with the
      registry hash); the paradigm CLI pin is a digest of its whole dist dir
      (pinParadigmCli, dirDigest). Builtins run as node <paradigm cli> loop
      builtin <name> --json and need a JSON verdict for that builtin: exit 2
      or no valid verdict is error builtin-error. A json rule's value is
      bounded (256 chars) and secret-scanned. Residual: node_modules
      dependencies of checks are not pinned (8.1: pin or ship builtins in
      Gnomon). Verdict: check-parse.ts pass rule (exit; tap, junit, tsc
      with failed as the allowed maximum; json JSONPath with == != < <= > >=);
      crash, timeout, spawn failure, sandbox refusal, unparseable report or
      output past 8 MiB is error. Evidence: stdout and stderr tails of 64 KiB
      through secret-scan.ts (AWS keys and secrets, GitHub, GitLab, npm,
      Stripe, Slack, Anthropic, OpenAI and Google tokens, JWTs, URL passwords, PEM private keys incl. cut blocks, bearer and
      generic api_key/secret/token/password assignments), labelled private and
      sensitive, envKeys names only. The run dir is removed after pass/fail,
      kept on error with a retention note; sweepCheckCopies removes kept dirs
      past the window. CheckExecInput.abort (#loop-runner's pause and kill
      switch) SIGKILLs the process group at once (then the setsid sweep): the
      check is error `killed`. Falsifiers: G-SBX-07, G-SBX-04..06 and G-SEC-02 against
      this profile (test/loop-check-sandbox.test.ts, darwin); parsers,
      scanner, env, registry and copy everywhere (test/loop-check.test.ts).
      TD-2026-10-23-268/448: kernel/loops/loop-env.ts reads a node's `env`
      values in the kernel: <home>/loop-env FIRST (owner-only 0600/0400, never
      a symlink), then the loop's envFiles; every open O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK,
      regular files <= 1 MiB, dotenv without expansion. A repo file must
      realpath inside the repo, nothing inside the Gnomon home but loop-env,
      no config-named file (isConfigNamedEnvFile); credential paths allowed.
      Errors env-refused, env-file (outside the repo: only "cannot be used:
      <path>") and env-missing (naming the files tried), all before any copy
      or spawn; the runner passes pre-read envValues and keeps them in memory
      for the run's redaction. The kernel's own git/copy spawns never get
      values; linked repo env files are never copied (skipFiles). redact:false
      keeps output raw; with redaction on values redact by literal
      ([REDACTED:env:NAME], URL-encoded too). network:on allows outbound IP
      and the mDNSResponder socket in the loop-check SBPL, and denies every
      kernel listener (web, mod proxies, local-model endpoints) on every
      address (remote ip "*:PORT").
    path: src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, sandbox, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-env-kernel.test.ts
      - test/loop-check.test.ts
      - test/loop-check-sandbox.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/check-parse.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/secret-scan.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/shebang.ts
      - src/kernel/sandbox/loop-check.ts
      - src/platform/darwin/loop-check-sbpl.ts
  loop-land:
    description: >-
      #loop-land (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.2 Landing, Conflicts,
      Commits, Revert signals, §3.3 effective autonomy at land, LL4, threat
      37; build plan step 11, LP6b; G-LOOP-07, G-LOOP-08, G-LOOP-09,
      G-LOOP-15, G-LOOP-24, G-LOOP-25). Mechanics in src/kernel/git/land.ts
      (landCommit): the per-repo in-process land lock (withLandLock) plus the
      human's own <gitDir>/index.lock taken O_CREAT|O_EXCL; refusals that land
      nothing: merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert/bisect in progress, index.lock,
      a human turn in flight (waitable), HEAD detached or on another branch
      (symbolic-ref), the branch moved since the run's base, a rename, a
      gitlink at or above a path, a .git component, unsafe names, case and
      NFD collisions with any index path, unmerged, assume-unchanged,
      skip-worktree or staged entries, an untracked or ignored add, any
      .gitattributes attribute, core.autocrlf/eol/splitIndex/sparseCheckout,
      and a worktree entry whose bytes or exec bit differ from the base blob.
      The land commit is re-made in the park repo with Gnomon Loop
      <loop+<id>@gnomon.invalid> and the trailers On-Behalf-Of, Trusted-By,
      Gnomon-Loop, Gnomon-Run, Loop-Authored-By (and Symbols) last
      (landMessage strips trailer-shaped lines); its objects are re-hashed in
      the kernel and written with hash-object -w, then rev-list --objects
      --missing=error. loop.landed is written (beforeLand) BEFORE any visible
      change. Files: DirGuard (every parent lstat'd a real dir, realpath
      exact, the dir inode held open and re-compared), the original moved
      aside and compared to the base blob again, the new bytes in a temp file
      (O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW, old permission bits kept, exec only per the blob
      mode) linked in with link() (never replaces); any mismatch rolls every
      placed file back. Then update-ref <ref> <new> <base> (CAS), HEAD
      re-checked, and the index copy (update-index --index-info on a private
      GIT_INDEX_FILE) written through the held lock. findRevert finds a later
      commit that restores the land's blobs (content) or names it (message).
      Orchestration in src/kernel/loops/land.ts (LoopLander): auto lands
      re-gated each attempt (trusted at the run's hash, runnable, not held,
      landDecision with the cap re-read, else the runner's propose card);
      approved lands need the trust only; L3/manual wait. Waitable refusals
      write loop.land.waiting once per reason and retry (timer and nudge on
      turn.result/session.exited) until land.maxWaitMin, then loop.parked
      {max-wait} and a toast; structural ones park at once; an undone land
      writes loop.land.waiting{undone, sha}. scanReverts writes ONE
      merge.reverted{landedRef, revertSha, detectedBy} per reverted loop
      commit, from one log --raw read (strictness scoring of it is 8.1, and
      message-only reverts must not drive it). Aegis review fixes: the new
      index is written fully into the held lock and verified (size, read
      back, trailer checksum or skipHash zeros) BEFORE the CAS, so only the
      rename follows it and a failed rename undoes the land (H1); the private
      index copy keeps the original's mtime rounded down (racy git, H2); a
      split index, an unreadable or crlf/attributes-setting global, XDG or
      system config refuse (the read-only queries see the human's HOME, M2);
      the kill switch parks every waiting land (parkAll, M3); a second
      loop.landed{phase:'done'} closes each land and the boot reconcile closes
      interrupted ones (stale index.lock, leftovers to
      <home>/loop-work/recovered/<runId>/, a toast) and parks lands waiting
      at the restart (M4); in-progress re-checked under the lock, the undo CAS
      checked (a conflict leaves the files), changed originals kept in
      recovered/, a branch checked out in another worktree refused, local
      attended sessions counted (L1-L7). 8.1: placement with openat/renameat
      in the helper (M5).
    path: src/kernel/loops/land.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, git, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-land.test.ts
      - test/loop-land-git.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-e2e.test.ts
      - test/loop-events.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/land.ts
      - src/kernel/git/land.ts
  loop-act:
    description: >-
      #loop-act (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 act role and tools, §3.3
      effective autonomy, §3.4 the clamp and protected goal inputs, §4
      Sandboxes; build plan step 10, LP6a; G-LOOP-22, the card half of
      G-LOOP-23). Act nodes (model role act, tools read and edit; bash is
      refused at validation until 8.1) run through LocalLane.openLoop(ref,
      {role:'act', act}) with the registry run reference (Aegis step 8: abort
      checks, live() re-checks, fenced inputs, closed {verdict, confidence,
      summary}), in the run's live scratch (the cwd and the ONE root allowed
      inside the Gnomon home), under the `loop-act` profile
      (src/kernel/sandbox/loop-act.ts, LOOP_ACT_BACKENDS = {darwin}; win32 and
      linux refuse sandbox-unavailable; src/platform/darwin/loop-act-sbpl.ts:
      the session SBPL plus a tail denying every connect but the loopback
      port of the loops:true endpoint, every exec but the runner node, every
      write but the scratch and its tmp, reads of the human repo but its
      node_modules, the Gnomon home but the run dir, keys and credential
      stores last). Tools come only from the trust card through the loop
      clamp (loop-clamp.ts loopActTools: loop-trust:<cardId>#tool-read|edit
      via loopClampAllows; no card, no tools, FORBIDDEN). The child
      (gnomon-local protect.ts) refuses a write to the protected set (the
      registry's compiled protected list: goal.inputs, the config closure,
      argv-named paths, callees'; plus ACT_ALWAYS_PROTECTED node_modules,
      .paradigm/loops, .gnomon) with a protected-input result and no card;
      the kernel reads it (LoopTurn.protectedHits) and the node fails
      (G-LOOP-22). Checks in an act run copy the scratch (CheckExecInput
      scratch). Propose (src/kernel/loops/propose.ts): after the run the
      scratch diff is parked (loop.parked) and becomes ONE verdict card
      (detail.origin loop, the kernel line "Raised by loop X (claimed by Y |
      from the loop file)", files, goal result, reasons, the diff) and
      loop.proposed; land hazards (a protected path, a tracked deletion, an
      added @ts-nocheck|@ts-ignore|eslint-disable|.skip|.only|xit, an
      unscanned diff, a symlink) always propose; 8.0 is APPROVE-TO-LAND
      (founder decision): autoLandBlockers returns "auto-land lands in 8.1"
      while LOOP_AUTO_LAND is false, so every change raises the card (the
      8.1 auto rules stay behind that seam: goal reached on a check, a land
      block covering every path, no decide before-land, no gate-class path,
      not held); the trust card of an act loop with a land block says
      "Proposes changes to your current branch; nothing lands until you
      accept the card."; L2 caps act to propose; observe edits nothing. The card is hand-only: accept lands it
      through #loop-land, reject drops the ref. A run stopped from outside
      parks and raises no card. Aegis review: the
      tail also denies fork, signals beyond itself, executable mapping of
      the scratch and tmp, deleting or renaming them, and reads of the git common
      dir and sibling worktrees; it re-denies the kernel's loopback ports
      after the endpoint allow (an endpoint on one is refused). The diff is
      parsed by structure; card text is escaped visibly; an
      execution-surface hazard class (hooks, CI, editor tasks, .envrc,
      .gitattributes, .gitmodules, package.json) always proposes. The runner
      awaits model.settled(runId) before parking. Escape suite:
      test/loop-act-sandbox.test.ts (darwin).
      TD-2026-10-23-268/448: the runner resolves an act node's `env` values
      from the loop's env files (a failure is the node's error, nothing opens)
      and hands them to LocalLane.openLoop, which refuses any name the trusted
      node does not declare (LoopRunRefInfo.nodeEnv, Aegis F4) and applies
      them after the dropped kernel names (applyLoopEnv). Model replies and
      summaries are redacted by literal (Aegis F2); loop.node.detail is always
      scanned (F5); a parked diff whose added lines carry a handed-out value
      is an env-value land hazard and the card diff shows it redacted (F3).
    path: src/kernel/loops/propose.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, sandbox, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-act.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-e2e.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-sandbox.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/propose.ts
      - src/kernel/sandbox/loop-act.ts
      - src/platform/darwin/loop-act-sbpl.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/local-session.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts
  loop-runner:
    description: >-
      #loop-runner (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 graph semantics, §3.3 kill
      switch and resume classes, §3.5 budgets, §4 rows, restart, concurrency;
      build plan step 5, LP3) in src/kernel/loops/{runner,ledger}.ts, wired
      only through kernel/loops/wire.ts. Walks a TRUSTED loop's compiled
      graph from `start` (registry.verify + canRun before the run and every
      node; a hash, closure, trust or pause change ends the run before its
      next node). check nodes run through check-exec runCheck with argv0 and
      its interpreter pinned from the trusted hash and the timeout cut to the
      run's remaining budget; notify `as: toast` writes loop.notified{as:
      toast, text} (kernel-field template: loop.id, run.id, round,
      failed.node, last.node, last.outcome; unknown fields render ?) and
      hands the toast to the optional GUI port; notify `as: card` raises a
      loop-choice card (continue/stop are the edge keys; no answer by the TTL
      takes `timeout`); call nodes check registry.resolveCall (trusted pin,
      depth ≤ 3) and run the callee as a child run whose meter is chained to
      the caller's (goal → pass, budget → unsure, else fail); model nodes
      (step 8, below); act nodes (model role act) run in a scratch
      worktree and end in a propose card since step 10 (#loop-act, #loop-git;
      a called loop with act nodes is refused); write checks (effect: write)
      are still refused at run start; effective
      autonomy = the loop's until step 9. Terminals @goal/@stop/@pause (a
      missing edge is @stop; @pause writes a kernel loop.paused{reason:
      timeout|unavailable|node-exit}); exit.maxVisits/maxFailStreak end the
      run node-exit. Budgets (LL3) checked before every step: run.rounds (a
      round closes when the walk revisits a node), run.wallMin,
      run.computeSec, day.runs and day.computeSec per loop, global dayRuns
      and dayComputeSec (LOOP_GLOBAL_CAPS); absent fields are the global
      defaults (effectiveBudgets). Rows (kernel principal): loop.run.started
      {runId, loopId, scope, repoKey, contentHash, trigger, budgets,
      parentRunId?}, loop.node{runId, nodeId, type, visit, round, verdict,
      edge, next, durationMs, evidenceRef, evidence (sensitive by the kind's
      label), effectiveAutonomy}, loop.round, loop.notified, loop.run.ended
      {reason, terminal?, rounds, computeMs}. The ledger (ledger.ts) folds
      them back from the log (LL6): open runs, recent runs, per-day counts
      from chain time; replayLoopRuns reproduces every edge and `gnomon
      replay` (log/replay.ts) folds it into projection.loops with a
      loop-edge-mismatch divergence (G-LOOP-17). Restart: recover() at boot
      closes every open run daemon-restart, nothing resumes mid-step
      (G-LOOP-14). Concurrency: one run per loop (a trigger during a run sets
      ONE rerun flag), caps.repo (2) and caps.global per repo and overall,
      more queue FIFO. Requests (human, gui or device): request.loop.run,
      request.loop.pause (a tightening: loop.paused{human}, the run aborted),
      request.loop.pause{all:true} (the kill switch: every run aborted, check
      process groups killed, one loop.paused{kill-switch} per trusted loop,
      no presence), request.loop.resume (after a human pause at once; after a
      kernel pause a hand-only loop-choice card whose continue, with fresh
      presence, writes loop.resumed{cardId}; content or closure changes need
      a new trust; Aegis M3). status.loops adds running, queued, caps and
      recent runs. CLI: gnomon loop run|pause [--all]|resume|runs|logs.
      Aegis step-5 fixes: the ledger's per-day, per-key counters are their
      own fold (record eviction never touches them) and a callee's compute
      is charged to the root caller's key (loop.run.started.rootKey), whose
      day.computeSec gates the chain; card nodes: timeout → @pause and stop
      → a terminal (schema CARD_EDGE and the runner), a card that cannot be
      raised ends the run @stop; the kill switch sets an in-memory flag and
      holds every paused key synchronously (start refuses until resumed); a
      run-budget stop writes loop.paused{budget} and drops the rerun flag;
      untrust and delete stop the run (trust.ts onRevoke); the per-node
      trust gate walks the caller chain; recover() skips live run ids; daemon
      exit aborts runs (no check outlives the kernel). The daemon pins the
      paradigm CLI for builtins once (wire.ts paradigmCliResolver: Node
      resolution of @a-company/paradigm from Gnomon, dist digest); not found,
      builtins fail closed.
      Step 8 (LP5, local model nodes; gnomon-loops.md §2, §3.4-§3.6) in
      src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts: judge and summarize steps open an
      UNATTENDED local session through the `model` port
      (supervisor.openLoop → LocalLane.openLoop), the one unattended open,
      reachable only with a run reference the registry mints for a TRUSTED,
      runnable loop (registry.runRef; module-private WeakMap,
      loopRunRefInfo; forged or released ⇒ FORBIDDEN before any row). The
      session: session.open{attended:false, origin:'loop', loopRun},
      surface 'none' (no tools), one step per turn, no GNOMON_SOCK /
      SESSION_KEY / HSID in its env (LL5: no paradigm_loop write op, no
      loop.request), its model text only hashed in turn.event. Prompt: the
      trusted template (kernel fields only), then each input as a fenced,
      receipted DATA block with a per-prompt nonce (inputs: a node's
      evidence on this run, last/failed evidence, loop/run fields, the
      trigger's kernel-stamped fields TRIGGER_FIELDS); the receipt rides
      session.inject{origin:'kernel', channel:'loop'} and loop.node
      evidence.inputs. Verdict: exactly one JSON object {verdict,
      confidence, summary?} from the closed enum (a judge's classes, first
      class = pass, others fail; summarize pass|fail); otherwise ONE repair
      turn, then `unsure`; confidence < minConfidence ⇒ unsure; nothing is
      searched for in prose (G-LOOP-06). Free text is secret-scanned and
      kept as the node's sensitive evidence (reply, summary). Availability:
      none configured, profile unmatched, endpoint down or incapable, no
      runner, a failed call or a timeout ⇒ LoopModelUnavailable, 3 retries
      with backoff, then the unavailable edge (compiled default @pause,
      loop.paused{unavailable}, a toast); never a fallback card, never
      Claude (G-LCL-02, G-LOOP-12 N/A in 8.0, G-LOOP-13). A model loop with
      no configured local model is refused at start (no-local-model).
      Budgets: each step's spawn permit has scope.loop = the root caller's
      key; the ledger folds permit.grant + budget.usage tokens by permitId
      into its per-day counters (day.tokens, global dayTokens); the run
      meter holds run.tokens; a node's limits.tokens caps the reply and an
      overrun is unsure. Thrash (k=3): in a loop with model or write steps,
      the same check-evidence hash three rounds in a row ends the run
      `thrash` with loop.paused{thrash} and a toast; resuming takes the
      hand-only loop-choice card. ^loop-origin-clamp
      (supervisor/loop-clamp.ts): in loop sessions only the loop's own
      trust rules auto-pass (none in 8.0), cards carry detail.origin 'loop'
      and the line "Raised by loop X (authored by Y)" (authoredBy is `file`
      until step 12's request rows); loop-choice cards carry it too.
      Aegis step-8 review (applied): no public mint (the registry issues
      its run-reference minter ONCE, taken by the runner, which vouches:
      live run, judge/summarize node, the walk's real root); live() also
      fails on the kill-switch hold and a strictness pause; openLoop closes
      the session and throws if the run aborted or stopped being live
      during the spawn, and nothing is asked once the signal is aborted;
      the supervisor permission path routes a loop session's mode,
      read-only and rule/always passes through loopClampAllows (exact
      `loop-trust:<cardId>[#rule]`) and its cards through withLoopOrigin
      (unreachable in 8.0; a source test fails if a pass skips mayAuto);
      prompts are budgeted before building (≤ 8 inputs, END markers and the
      answer line intact, receipts hash what is sent); verdict reasons never
      echo model text; the thrash streak carries across runs
      (loop.round{evidenceHash} folded in the ledger, reset by a thrash
      end); only endpoints marked `loops: true` in the root-signed local
      config serve loops (the runner refuses at start otherwise).
    path: src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, security]
    gates: [^loop-trusted, ^loop-origin-clamp]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-runner.test.ts
      - test/loop-runner-darwin.test.ts
      - test/loop-model.test.ts
      - test/loop-model-e2e.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/ledger.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
      - src/cli/loop-cli.ts
  loop-timers:
    description: >-
      #loop-timers (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §4 scheduler integration and
      uptime hold, §10 D1; build plan step 6, LP4a) in
      src/kernel/loops/timers.ts, built by wire.ts when the daemon passes its
      scheduler. Reconciles on every registry change (scan, folded trust row)
      and runner event: each schedule trigger of a RUNNABLE loop (trusted at
      its current hash, unpaused, not held by the kill switch) has one
      scheduler timer (loop:<loopKey>#<digest>), every other loop none, so a
      fresh install has zero loop timers (G-LAW-05b). daily/weekly times are
      read in the daemon's zone (the host's IANA zone; the loop:v2 schema
      states no tz yet); every is anchored at the loop's trust or resume
      instant (registry.runnableSince), and fires at or before it are never
      owed. Continuous: the next run is a one-shot timer at end + cooldown
      (≥ 60 s), at boot, trust or resume now + cooldown; a paused loop (a
      budget stop pauses), a killed one or a refused start (no next run that
      local day) gets none. Kill switch: the runner's kill event drops every
      loop timer synchronously before the pause rows (G-LOOP-26). Uptime
      (D1): while any loop timer has a next due instant or a run is in
      flight, the `loop` idle holder keeps the daemon up; status.loops.uptime
      {enabled, running, waiting: {n, of}, keepingAlive, line: "Gnomon is
      staying up for loops", nextDueAt}. The wrapper's window close already
      hides the window and keeps the kernel (gnomon-wrapper main.rs
      CloseRequested); only Cmd-Q stops it.
    path: src/kernel/loops/timers.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, scheduler, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-timers.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/timers.ts
      - src/kernel/scheduler/zoned.ts
      - src/kernel/scheduler/scheduler.ts
      - src/kernel/lifecycle/idle.ts
  loop-events:
    description: >-
      #loop-events (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 triggers, §4 #loop-events;
      build plan step 7, LP4b) in src/kernel/loops/events.ts, built by
      wire.ts (one marked block). Event triggers for RUNNABLE loops only
      (trusted at the current hash, unpaused, not held by the kill switch),
      reconciled on every registry change and runner event like
      #loop-timers: an untrusted, paused or killed loop has no watch and no
      subscription. file.changed: one recursive fs.watch per repo (realpath
      root), matched against the compiled flow globs (trigger.paths or
      flowPaths); .git, node_modules and the Gnomon home (check copies in
      loop-work) ignored; a path whose realpath leaves the repo is refused
      (loops.event-refused symlink-escape). commit.landed: a watch on the git
      dir (HEAD, refs/heads, packed-refs; worktree gitdir/commondir), parsed
      directly (the kernel runs no git); a new sha on the same branch fires,
      a branch switch is a new baseline. session.ended: kernel
      session.exited rows whose session.open cwd is inside the loop's repo.
      card.decided: kernel approval.decide rows for a card a run of {loop,
      node} raised (loop.notified), same repo. Matching reads kernel-stamped
      fields only. Debounce is trailing per (loop, trigger), at least 250 ms
      for watch events, capped at 3x the debounce. Each fire hands off to
      runner.start FIRST (trust, kill, strictness, day caps) with kernel ids
      only {type:'event', kind, fireId, count, sha|hsid|cardId} (never path or
      ref, which could reach a model prompt); only a run that starts or
      queues writes loop.trigger.fired{loopId, scope, repoKey, trigger{...,
      path|ref}, eventId?}; a coalesced or refused fire is a per-loop counter
      (dropped()), not a row. Replay (G-LOOP-17): ledger.ts replayLoopRuns
      joins each run to its row by fireId over the whole history (`why`); a
      run citing an unknown or foreign fireId diverges. Git dirs are
      validated as git links them (worktree gitdir back-link, commondir);
      only HEAD, packed-refs and refs/heads are watched. Self-trigger guard
      (G-LOOP-15): an event whose causes chain (runId, cardId, loop session
      hsid, causes refs ev:/card:/hsid: transitively, parentRunId callers)
      includes a run of L never triggers L; a commit is a loop's own only
      when a kernel loop.landed{sha} row names it (land writes it before
      moving the ref);
      a file change in a loop's land.paths while it runs, or in a
      loop.landed{paths} row for 60 s, is its own. sentinel.incident.opened
      is refused at validation (8.1).
    path: src/kernel/loops/events.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-events.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-15, G-LOOP-17]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/events.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/ledger.ts
      - src/kernel/log/replay.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
  loop-strictness:
    description: >-
      #loop-strictness (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.3; build plan step 9,
      M2.11-lite) in src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts: a loop's EFFECTIVE
      autonomy = min(its declared autonomy, the repo cap {level, effective run
      mode}), re-read at run start, before every node and on every loop.node
      row (Aegis M2), with a capReason for the GUI ("act → propose (L2)",
      status.loops.effective). L3 or manual: no run starts
      (FORBIDDEN{strictness-paused}) and a running loop pauses before its next
      node (loop.paused{reason:'strictness'}, a kernel pause: resuming takes
      fresh presence). L2: act runs as propose and lands nothing alone;
      concurrency 1. L1: concurrency 2 per repo (spec strictness.concurrency.L1); gate-class and kernel-high-risk
      lands need the second reviewer (8.1), so landDecision() makes them propose
      with secondReviewer recorded. L0: as declared. The repo brake
      (wire.ts brake(repoKey)) pauses the repo's running loops at once on a
      level raise or a repo run-mode tightening. A missing or throwing cap
      source is L3 (fail safe). Step 11's land calls landDecision() with the cap
      re-read.
    path: src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, strictness, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-strictness.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-10]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
  loop-agent-ops:
    description: >-
      #loop-agent-ops (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §5 "What an agent may do",
      §3.1 Attribution; build plan step 12, LP7) in
      src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts: the kernel pre-gate for an AGENT's
      userland loop.request{op, loopId, …}, routed by one dispatch.ts block
      (wire.ts port.agent). The asker's principal and hsid come from the
      connection, never the payload (Aegis). Targets are repos the registry
      already knows (never addRepo on this path); unknown repo and unknown loop
      are one NOT_FOUND; rows carry the registry's repo root. run: trusted
      loops only. pause: any loop (loop.paused{reason:'agent', by}); a loop it
      did not author toasts the founder. resume: only its own agent pause made
      in the current kill epoch (pauseAll writes one loop.paused{all:true}
      epoch row), then ONE catch-up run if a schedule or continuous trigger
      came due during the pause. delete: in 8.0 always a pause plus a founder
      toast. Reads (runs, logs, trust-status) are scoped to a named repo and
      redacted (verdicts and counts, no evidence or node detail, no row).
      create/update with sourceHash writes a kernel loop.request row (repo is
      a sensitive field) the registry folds into authoredBy{principal, hsid,
      basis:'agent-key-claim'} for that source hash (else `file`), and
      raises the loop's ONE loop-trust card (requested by the agent, decided
      by the human with the root key; no envelope in 8.0). Session-class
      askers and loop sessions (supervisor.isLoopSession, LL5) are refused
      every op; peers, voice, mods, read tokens and humans too (humans use
      request.loop.*). src/agent/index.ts (@a-company/gnomon/agent) is the
      agent principal's client for paradigm-mcp (GNOMON_AGENT_KEY, which must
      resolve inside the Gnomon home, or <home>/agent/device.key, with a
      root-signed agent registry row). Residual: no peer-pid or
      connection-to-session mapping exists, so loop sessions are recognised
      only by a connection-bound hsid (none bound until session-key auth);
      they hold today because loop sessions get no socket or session key.
    path: src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, agents, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-agent-ops.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-03, G-LOOP-05]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/registry.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/trust.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
      - src/kernel/socket/dispatch.ts
      - src/agent/index.ts
